WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.589
The math inside stretto, as the code computes it.

2
00:00:14.089 --> 00:00:18.384
One event to estimate, one rule to act on it, and the counts between.

3
00:00:20.184 --> 00:00:24.200
A lookup pays off if the agent uses it before its next write.

4
00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:30.750
So stretto estimates q: the chance a read is among those the agent makes before it writes.

5
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.844
That chance is at least the chance the read comes next, and often far larger.

6
00:00:36.294 --> 00:00:42.214
After a user's details, the habit put an order next at 0.64.

7
00:00:42.214 --> 00:00:46.264
The agents read one before the write 94% of the time.

8
00:00:47.964 --> 00:00:51.949
A flow makes only reads, and reads leave the state as it was.

9
00:00:52.399 --> 00:00:56.772
If the agent makes the same calls, every write lands on the same state.

10
00:00:57.222 --> 00:01:00.822
A wrong lookup changes only what the agent reads.

11
00:01:00.822 --> 00:01:02.467
Its worst case is a detour.

12
00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:07.527
Used, a lookup saves a turn, worth beta.

13
00:01:07.527 --> 00:01:09.813
Unused, it costs a detour, delta.

14
00:01:10.263 --> 00:01:15.738
Its expected value is q times beta, minus one minus q, times delta.

15
00:01:16.188 --> 00:01:21.084
That's positive exactly when q clears delta over beta plus delta.

16
00:01:21.634 --> 00:01:28.899
Live on τ²-bench, beta was about 6,000 input tokens and delta about 2,530.

17
00:01:29.349 --> 00:01:32.329
So the line sits at about 0.3.

18
00:01:32.329 --> 00:01:35.361
In airline and telecom, the counts put it lower.

19
00:01:37.161 --> 00:01:39.461
The chances come from counts.

20
00:01:39.461 --> 00:01:43.991
Each step becomes its tool, its outcome, and a feature of its result.

21
00:01:44.441 --> 00:01:47.802
A back-off model predicts the next step from the last two.

22
00:01:48.202 --> 00:01:54.281
Each context is a Dirichlet–multinomial, with the shorter context as its prior mean.

23
00:01:54.731 --> 00:02:00.065
For the reach decider, the same contexts count each read used before the next write.

24
00:02:01.765 --> 00:02:04.865
Alpha sets how fast the counts outweigh the prior.

25
00:02:04.865 --> 00:02:06.328
It isn't tuned by hand.

26
00:02:06.778 --> 00:02:11.431
It's inferred, with fugue, a probabilistic programming library for Rust.

27
00:02:11.881 --> 00:02:14.781
The program draws log alpha from a normal prior,

28
00:02:14.781 --> 00:02:18.538
and scores the episodes, one step at a time.

29
00:02:18.988 --> 00:02:24.025
Metropolis–Hastings samples the posterior, and the flow keeps its median.

30
00:02:25.825 --> 00:02:28.665
A lookup also needs its arguments.

31
00:02:28.665 --> 00:02:30.682
stretto counts where each one came from.

32
00:02:31.132 --> 00:02:35.006
A binding's chance is right over tried, shrunk toward one half.

33
00:02:35.456 --> 00:02:40.133
The decision multiplies the two: the read's chance, times its arguments'.

34
00:02:41.833 --> 00:02:45.444
Counting the right event calibrates the probability of use.

35
00:02:45.894 --> 00:02:54.090
In replay, its calibration error was 0.01 to 0.08 in every τ²-bench domain.

36
00:02:54.540 --> 00:03:00.312
Scored on the next step instead, it was 0.06 to 0.16.

37
00:03:02.212 --> 00:03:04.636
A flow is audited as a program too.

38
00:03:05.036 --> 00:03:08.916
Each decision is a categorical choice at its own address:

39
00:03:08.916 --> 00:03:11.563
a lookup, handing back, or another step.

40
00:03:12.013 --> 00:03:17.330
fugue scores the agent's real path under it, in nats of surprise per decision.

41
00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:23.070
The paper gives the proofs and the benchmark numbers.

42
00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:25.267
The worked examples are the walkthrough's.
